From 2a61eebe4546cf0b0e446968987a6496be876c01 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Illya Antonyuk <illantalex84@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 23:51:43 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 32/49] ALSA: arm: pnx4008-dsp: prime the DSP with silence on
 start

Taking two periods at trigger time ran the position ahead of an application
that starts after one period (mpg123): immediate underrun, and mpg123 gave
up on the short write. Prime both halves with silence and take data only
on the DSP's requests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sound/arm/pnx4008-dsp.c | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/arm/pnx4008-dsp.c b/sound/arm/pnx4008-dsp.c
index c92377dbb..695ff2cd8 100644
--- a/sound/arm/pnx4008-dsp.c
+++ b/sound/arm/pnx4008-dsp.c
@@ -178,12 +178,14 @@ static int pnx4008_dsp_trigger(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream, int cmd)
 		spin_lock(&dsp->lock);
 		/*
 		 * The DSP waits with its request pending since the last half it took (or
-		 * since Symbian's last one): drop that request and give it a full pair.
+		 * since Symbian's last one): drop that request and prime both halves with
+		 * silence. Data is only taken on the DSP's requests, so the position never
+		 * runs ahead of what the application has written (it may start after a
+		 * single period).
 		 */
 		apbp_wr(dsp, APBP_PCLEAR, STREAM_SEM);
 		dsp->half = 0;
-		pnx4008_dsp_fill(dsp, substream->runtime);
-		pnx4008_dsp_fill(dsp, substream->runtime);
+		memset_io(dsp->area, 0, 2 * HALF_BYTES);
 		pnx4008_dsp_psem(dsp, STREAM_SEM);
 		dsp->running = true;
 		spin_unlock(&dsp->lock);
